  "narrative": {
    "intro": "World Wide Technology has $337M in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date across 274 awards, with an average award size of $1.2M. Obligations are down 34.0% year over year. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, having obligated $280M so far, followed by the Department of Justice at $53.5M and the Smithsonian Institution at $1.6M. Other notable agency obligations include the Department of Energy at $642K and the Department of Health and Human Services at $341K. Other Computer Related Services is the largest market for World Wide Technology, with $226M obligated, followed by Software Publishers at $64M and Electronic Computer Manufacturing at $22.9M. Fiscal-year comparisons show prior obligations of $497M in FY21, $495M in FY22, $468M in FY23, $483M in FY24, and $510M in FY25, before the current-year total of $337M. Current-year totals are partial and represent fiscal year to date activity.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Why did obligations fall 34.0% year over year?",
        "body": "Year-over-year change shows obligations are down 34.0% in FY2026 to date. Fiscal-year comparisons show obligations of $510M in FY25 and $337M in FY26, which illustrates the decline in absolute terms. The Department of Defense remains the primary buyer with $280M obligated in FY2026 to date, which still composes the majority of current-year obligations. Other agencies together account for the remaining obligations, with the Department of Justice at $53.5M and smaller amounts from civilian agencies."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Where are World Wide Technology's federal dollars concentrated?",
        "body": "Other Computer Related Services is the largest market, with $226M in obligations in FY2026 to date. Software Publishers and Electronic Computer Manufacturing follow, with $64M and $22.9M respectively. The Department of Defense is the largest buying agency with $280M, followed by the Department of Justice at $53.5M and the Smithsonian Institution at $1.6M."
      }
    ]
  },
